<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Azes I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 58–35 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Azes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Azes I was an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythian</a> king of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the 1st century BCE who ruled over Gandhara and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. He is widely thought to have founded the Azes era, which began in about 57 BCE and was used in later inscriptions, though its exact start is debated. His abundant coins continued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek</a> bilingual designs.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/azes-i.jpg" alt="Azes I" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Uploadalt · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Azes_I.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Indo-Greeks were finally conquered by the Indo-Scythians: Maues ruled briefly in the northwest around 80 BCE, <b>Azes I</b> defeated Hippostratus in 48/47 BCE, and Rajuvula took the last kingdom around 10 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>There Indo-Scythian kings such as Maues and <b>Azes</b> ruled in the first century BCE, and Saka dynasties of satraps lasted for centuries: the Western Kshatrapas governed Gujarat and Malwa until about 400 CE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saka">Saka · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The reliquaries use several eras, including the <b>Azes</b> era and an era of the Greeks, and are important evidence for the chronology of the period.</blockquote><a href="/state/apracharajas">Apracharajas</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Azes I</b>, whose era beginning in 57 BCE was used in inscriptions, the kingdom spread over Gandhara and the Punjab.</blockquote><a href="/state/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythians</a></li></ul></section>
